DSE often gives global data and asks for small Boolean sub-programs that read it, then combines them. Example: global username and N (its length).<pre>def hasDigit():
    for i in range(0, N):
        if username[i] &gt;= "0" and username[i] &lt;= "9":
            return True
    return False

def noSpace():
    for i in range(0, N):
        if username[i] == " ":
            return False
    return True

def checkExist():
    for i in range(0, len(user)):
        if username == user[i]:
            return True
    return False

def checkUsername():
    return hasDigit() and noSpace() and not checkExist()</pre>Pattern: return as soon as the answer is known inside the loop; the final return after the loop handles "not found". Note <code>return False</code> is <b>outside</b> the loop.

Stubs are sub-programs too, so we only define the <b>sub-program name</b> and, if needed, a fixed <b>return value</b>:<pre># stub with no return value
def stubs_with_no_return_value():
    print()      # or: pass

# stub with a return value
def stubs_with_return_value():
    return 100   # change to suit the return data type</pre>The fixed value should have the <b>right data type</b> and be a sensible test value, e.g. a stub for calcBMI returns 21 (a float/int), a stub for analyse returns "Normal" (a string). Keep the same name and parameters as the real sub-program so the main program does not need changing.

