Input and output devices are also called <b>external/peripheral devices</b>. They are controlled by the device's "brain": the <b>programmable microcontroller</b>, the <b>main processor on an electronic device</b>. It is usually a <b>small circuit board with various ports and pins</b>, which allow users to freely connect different input and output devices. Examples: <b>Arduino, micro:bit, Raspberry Pi</b>.<br><br>A complete electronic device has <b>hardware</b> (the physical parts: board, sensors, LEDs) and <b>software</b> (the program). The user <b>uploads a program</b> to the microcontroller, which then <b>reads</b> input devices and <b>controls</b> output devices according to the instructions.<br><br>Example Arduino code: <pre>void loop() {
  if (analogRead(A5) > 400) digitalWrite(10, HIGH);
  else digitalWrite(10, LOW);
}</pre> reads a sensor on pin A5 and switches an LED on pin 10.

In <b>event-driven programming</b>, the programmer sets the <b>event</b> that triggers program execution; <b>once the specified event occurs, the corresponding code (event handler) is executed</b>. Daily examples: press a camera shutter → take a photo; press a lift button → lift comes.<br><br>micro:bit (Lab 8.3):<pre>on button A pressed  → show string "A"
on button B pressed  → show string "B"
on button A+B pressed → clear screen
on shake → steps ← steps + 1</pre><br>Compare with a <b>forever loop</b>, which keeps checking (polling) a condition again and again. Event handlers wait until something happens. In Python we can simulate this with a <b>dictionary mapping event names to handler functions</b>:<pre>handlers = {'A': lambda: print('A'), 'B': lambda: print('B')}
for e in ['A', 'B', 'A']:
    handlers[e]()</pre>

